\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/><line x1=\"222.45999908447266\" y1=\"116\" x2=\"279.4759994506836\" y2=\"140.44444444444443\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#marker0)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker0)\"/><line x1=\"222.45999908447266\" y1=\"220.44444444444446\" x2=\"279.4759994506836\" y2=\"227.11111111111111\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 6\" marker-end=\"url(#marker1)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker1)\"/><line x1=\"222.45999908447266\" y1=\"267.1111111111111\" x2=\"279.4759994506836\" y2=\"279.3333333333333\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-end=\"url(#marker2)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/><line x1=\"279.4759994506836\" y1=\"140.44444444444443\" x2=\"336.4919998168946\" y2=\"151.55555555555557\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#marker0)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker0)\"/><line x1=\"279.4759994506836\" y1=\"227.11111111111111\" x2=\"336.4919998168946\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 6\" marker-end=\"url(#marker1)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker1)\"/><line x1=\"279.4759994506836\" y1=\"279.3333333333333\" x2=\"336.4919998168946\" y2=\"266\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-end=\"url(#marker2)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for line graph titled Percentage of Maize Exported by Three Countries for Marketing Years 2009/2010–2013/2014\"><ul>\n<li>The following 3 lines are shown:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Argentina</li>\n<li>Brazil</li>\n<li>United States</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Argentina line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 2009/2010, 66.5%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2010/2011, 66%</li>\n<li>Rises sharply to 2011/2012, 81%</li>\n<li>Falls sharply to 2012/2013, 70%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2013/2014, 65%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Brazil line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 2009/2010, 21%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2010/2011, 15%</li>\n<li>Rises sharply to 2011/2012, 34%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2012/2013, 31%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2013/2014, 27%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The United States line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 2009/2010, 15%</li>\n<li>Remains level to 2010/2011, 15%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2011/2012, 13%</li>\n<li>Falls gradually to 2012/2013, 7.5%</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 2013/2014, 13.5%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Argentina, Brazil, and the United States are among the world&rsquo;s leading producers of maize (corn), and each country exports a certain percentage of maize each marketing year, which runs from March to February in Argentina and Brazil and from September to August in the United States. A student is researching those percentages and finds that for the marketing year 2012/2013, the percentage of maize exported by <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0a298e2d-39b2-4e8b-b310-6f55356a374f","content":"<p>Brazil increased from the previous marketing year but remained lower than the percentage exported by the United States.</p>"},{"id":"f46af1d4-51dc-4d19-9774-11798e8ee04c","content":"<p>Brazil exceeded the percentage exported by Argentina for the first time.</p>"},{"id":"1bee7d0f-8f3a-46a1-81c2-964385aab93a","content":"<p>Argentina decreased from the previous marketing year but remained the highest among the three countries.</p>"},{"id":"3fb1094f-f763-48fd-a06b-3a14a4ad01e2","content":"<p>the United States reached its highest point during the five marketing years.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["1bee7d0f-8f3a-46a1-81c2-964385aab93a"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text&rsquo;s discussion of the percentages of maize exported in the marketing year 2012/2013. The graph presents percentages of maize exported by Argentina, Brazil, and the United States in marketing years 2009/2010 to 2013/2014 and indicates that for the marketing year 2012/2013, the percentage of maize exported by Argentina decreased to about 70 percent from about 80 percent in the previous marketing year. The graph also shows that the percentage of maize exported by Argentina remained highest among the three countries in the marketing year 2012/2013, surpassing the percentage exported by Brazil (about 31 percent) and by the United States (about 8 percent).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because for the marketing year 2012/2013, the graph indicates that the percentage of maize exported by Brazil didn&rsquo;t increase from the previous year; rather, it decreased from about 34 percent to about 31 percent. Moreover, the graph shows that the percentage of maize exported by Brazil remained higher, not lower, than the percentage exported by the United States in the marketing year 2012/2013. Choice B is incorrect because the graph indicates that the percentage of maize exported by Brazil never exceeded the percentage exported by Argentina for any of the marketing years represented. Choice D is incorrect because the graph indicates that the percentage of maize exported by the United States reached its lowest point, not its highest, during the five marketing years in 2012/2013, with the United States exporting only about 8 percent of its maize in that marketing year.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323047,"updateDate":1730147323047}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323047, 1730147323047),
    ($SATQ$16025337$SATQ$, $SATQ$b549c4fb-32ff-4538-9eee-d2a99127f351$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7221ad5b-7058-4457-966b-f735c26cb33e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from William Shakespeare&rsquo;s 1609 poem &ldquo;Sonnet 27.&rdquo; The poem is addressed to a close friend as if he were physically present.</p>
<div style='padding-left:1em'><p>Weary with toil, I [hurry] to my bed,</p>
<p>The dear repose for limbs with travel tired;</p>
<p>But then begins a journey in my head</p>
<p>To work my mind, when body&rsquo;s work&rsquo;s expired:</p>
<p>For then my thoughts&mdash;from far where I abide&mdash;</p>
<p>[Begin] a zealous pilgrimage to thee,</p>
<p>And keep my drooping eyelids open wide,</p></div>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>What is the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7611e23c-d1c8-47d9-8732-c3180e93cc7e","content":"<p>The speaker is asleep and dreaming about traveling to see the friend.</p>"},{"id":"d5e01828-0c3d-4bcd-b4bc-085ed178f835","content":"<p>The speaker is planning an upcoming trip to the friend&rsquo;s house.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b265e57d-adbd-4cd8-8d46-c0b85f48e64b","content":"<p>The speaker is too fatigued to continue a discussion with the friend.</p>"},{"id":"73e9ab53-248e-4e65-bfbe-bed048b98e11","content":"<p>The speaker is thinking about the friend instead of immediately falling asleep.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["73e9ab53-248e-4e65-bfbe-bed048b98e11"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The speaker describes going to bed for &ldquo;repose&rdquo; (rest) but finding that his thoughts are focused on the friend the speaker is addressing, and the thoughts are keeping the speaker awake. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the speaker isn&rsquo;t asleep; the thoughts about the friend are keeping the speaker awake. Choice B is incorrect because the speaker isn&rsquo;t talking about taking a literal trip; rather, the speaker uses the metaphor of a journey to describe internal thoughts. Choice C is incorrect because&nbsp;the speaker isn&rsquo;t having a discussion with the friend. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-007-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9195c91c-a239-4f4e-91c7-04e0884e8955$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"16025337","external_id":"b549c4fb-32ff-4538-9eee-d2a99127f351","vaultid":"7221ad5b-7058-4457-966b-f735c26cb33e","uId":"9017e879-2acf-4c1b-857b-ccdccdfd2f29","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from William Shakespeare&rsquo;s 1609 poem &ldquo;Sonnet 27.&rdquo; The poem is addressed to a close friend as if he were physically present.</p>\n<div style='padding-left:1em'><p>Weary with toil, I [hurry] to my bed,</p>\n<p>The dear repose for limbs with travel tired;</p>\n<p>But then begins a journey in my head</p>\n<p>To work my mind, when body&rsquo;s work&rsquo;s expired:</p>\n<p>For then my thoughts&mdash;from far where I abide&mdash;</p>\n<p>[Begin] a zealous pilgrimage to thee,</p>\n<p>And keep my drooping eyelids open wide,</p></div>","stem":"<p>What is the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7611e23c-d1c8-47d9-8732-c3180e93cc7e","content":"<p>The speaker is asleep and dreaming about traveling to see the friend.</p>"},{"id":"d5e01828-0c3d-4bcd-b4bc-085ed178f835","content":"<p>The speaker is planning an upcoming trip to the friend&rsquo;s house.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b265e57d-adbd-4cd8-8d46-c0b85f48e64b","content":"<p>The speaker is too fatigued to continue a discussion with the friend.</p>"},{"id":"73e9ab53-248e-4e65-bfbe-bed048b98e11","content":"<p>The speaker is thinking about the friend instead of immediately falling asleep.</p>"}],"keys":["73e9ab53-248e-4e65-bfbe-bed048b98e11"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The speaker describes going to bed for &ldquo;repose&rdquo; (rest) but finding that his thoughts are focused on the friend the speaker is addressing, and the thoughts are keeping the speaker awake. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the speaker isn&rsquo;t asleep; the thoughts about the friend are keeping the speaker awake. Choice B is incorrect because the speaker isn&rsquo;t talking about taking a literal trip; rather, the speaker uses the metaphor of a journey to describe internal thoughts. Choice C is incorrect because&nbsp;the speaker isn&rsquo;t having a discussion with the friend. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-007-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9195c91c-a239-4f4e-91c7-04e0884e8955","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959313,"updateDate":1691007959313}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959313, 1691007959313),
    ($SATQ$16a4a83b$SATQ$, $SATQ$d8fe6696-8c72-4aef-ab66-1dbe2b5ac66c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$08446fa4-0deb-4e72-822a-5c614a609acd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>An Ideal Husband</em> is an 1895 play by Oscar Wilde. In the play, which is a satire, Wilde suggests that a character named Lady Gertrude Chiltern is perceived as both extremely virtuous and unforgiving, as is evident when another character says <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from <em>An Ideal Husband</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"84b3cc18-577a-4044-986a-41be78906e09","content":"<p>&ldquo;Lady Chiltern is a woman of the very highest principles, I am glad to say. I am a little too old now, myself, to trouble about setting a good example, but I always admire people who do.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"e5ae2bc3-1a70-44fe-ae8c-6635eb55dea6","content":"<p>&ldquo;Do you know, [Lady Chiltern], I don&rsquo;t mind your talking morality a bit. Morality is simply the attitude we adopt towards people whom we personally dislike.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"d7813110-e842-41c1-b7ed-a227328c6c97","content":"<p>&ldquo;[Lady Chiltern] does not know what weakness or temptation is. I am of clay like other men. She stands apart as good women do&mdash;pitiless in her perfection&mdash;cold and stern and without mercy.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7eb16fd8-6985-4f7f-9d5b-0d92c9b2585a","content":"<p>&ldquo;Lady Chiltern, you are a sensible woman, the most sensible woman in London, the most sensible woman I know.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d7813110-e842-41c1-b7ed-a227328c6c97"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses a quotation to illustrate the claim that Lady Gertrude Chiltern is perceived as &ldquo;both extremely virtuous and unforgiving.&rdquo; In the quotation, a man describes Lady Chiltern as someone who &ldquo;does not know what weakness or temptation is.&rdquo; In other words, the man regards her as someone who is strong and adheres to a strict definition of moral perfection. However, he ironically suggests that this definition excludes mercy and forgiveness&mdash;qualities that are also thought of as virtues; according to him, Lady Chiltern is &ldquo;pitiless in her perfection&mdash;cold and stern and without mercy.&rdquo; This description supports the idea that Lady Chiltern is perceived by others as virtuous as well as unforgiving. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The quotation supports the claim that Lady Chiltern is perceived as virtuous, in that it describes her as &ldquo;a woman of the very highest principles.&rdquo; However, it doesn&rsquo;t characterize her as unforgiving or being perceived as such. Choice B is incorrect. The quotation suggests that Lady Chiltern is concerned with morality, but it suggests that her interest in discussing it is fundamentally hypocritical and functions as a means by which to judge others. However, the quotation doesn&rsquo;t address the question of whether Lady Chiltern is unmerciful to those who seek forgiveness for harm they have caused. Choice D is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t address either Lady Chiltern&rsquo;s perceived virtuousness or her perceived lack of forgiveness; instead, it expresses the belief that she is sensible. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-416-LIT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c733a649-6c78-44c1-8802-0bbd7bce2ad1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"16a4a83b","external_id":"d8fe6696-8c72-4aef-ab66-1dbe2b5ac66c","vaultid":"08446fa4-0deb-4e72-822a-5c614a609acd","uId":"b4aac181-6e50-4b28-858a-ee1feaf85914","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>An Ideal Husband</em> is an 1895 play by Oscar Wilde. In the play, which is a satire, Wilde suggests that a character named Lady Gertrude Chiltern is perceived as both extremely virtuous and unforgiving, as is evident when another character says <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from <em>An Ideal Husband</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"84b3cc18-577a-4044-986a-41be78906e09","content":"<p>&ldquo;Lady Chiltern is a woman of the very highest principles, I am glad to say. I am a little too old now, myself, to trouble about setting a good example, but I always admire people who do.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"e5ae2bc3-1a70-44fe-ae8c-6635eb55dea6","content":"<p>&ldquo;Do you know, [Lady Chiltern], I don&rsquo;t mind your talking morality a bit. Morality is simply the attitude we adopt towards people whom we personally dislike.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"d7813110-e842-41c1-b7ed-a227328c6c97","content":"<p>&ldquo;[Lady Chiltern] does not know what weakness or temptation is. I am of clay like other men. She stands apart as good women do&mdash;pitiless in her perfection&mdash;cold and stern and without mercy.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7eb16fd8-6985-4f7f-9d5b-0d92c9b2585a","content":"<p>&ldquo;Lady Chiltern, you are a sensible woman, the most sensible woman in London, the most sensible woman I know.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["d7813110-e842-41c1-b7ed-a227328c6c97"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses a quotation to illustrate the claim that Lady Gertrude Chiltern is perceived as &ldquo;both extremely virtuous and unforgiving.&rdquo; In the quotation, a man describes Lady Chiltern as someone who &ldquo;does not know what weakness or temptation is.&rdquo; In other words, the man regards her as someone who is strong and adheres to a strict definition of moral perfection. However, he ironically suggests that this definition excludes mercy and forgiveness&mdash;qualities that are also thought of as virtues; according to him, Lady Chiltern is &ldquo;pitiless in her perfection&mdash;cold and stern and without mercy.&rdquo; This description supports the idea that Lady Chiltern is perceived by others as virtuous as well as unforgiving. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The quotation supports the claim that Lady Chiltern is perceived as virtuous, in that it describes her as &ldquo;a woman of the very highest principles.&rdquo; However, it doesn&rsquo;t characterize her as unforgiving or being perceived as such. Choice B is incorrect. The quotation suggests that Lady Chiltern is concerned with morality, but it suggests that her interest in discussing it is fundamentally hypocritical and functions as a means by which to judge others. However, the quotation doesn&rsquo;t address the question of whether Lady Chiltern is unmerciful to those who seek forgiveness for harm they have caused. Choice D is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t address either Lady Chiltern&rsquo;s perceived virtuousness or her perceived lack of forgiveness; instead, it expresses the belief that she is sensible. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-416-LIT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c733a649-6c78-44c1-8802-0bbd7bce2ad1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959336,"updateDate":1691007959336}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959336, 1691007959336),
    ($SATQ$16ce90d9$SATQ$, $SATQ$5d11fe17-72bb-49af-a3da-3be214ae0c9a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2018, scientists discovered an immense aggregation of <em>Muusoctopus robustus</em> (pearl octopuses) along a hydrothermal vent 3,200 meters beneath the ocean&rsquo;s surface. Water temperatures at this site&mdash;named the Octopus Garden&mdash;climb as high as 11&deg;C, much warmer than the ambient 1.6&deg;C typical at this depth. Based on observations made over three years, scientists concluded that temperatures at the site likely confer reproductive benefits and that the site is used exclusively for reproduction&mdash;6,000<em> <span data-ssml-sub-alias="M">M.</span> robustus</em> adults, hatchlings, and eggs were observed at the garden, but no juveniles were present.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which statement about <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias="M">M.</span> robustus</em> and the Octopus Garden is best supported by the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"35dd9554-2057-4232-a827-fbf3b54eedde","content":"<p><em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus </em>leave the Octopus Garden upon reaching an intermediary stage of development.</p>"},{"id":"1230a71b-5811-4147-87a8-49a3c3b5896e","content":"<p>The<em> <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus </em>population at the Octopus Garden remains stable despite variations in water temperature.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3e2a86d1-6489-472a-8b1f-7a522c8525bc","content":"<p><em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus</em> nests in the Octopus Garden contain on average fewer but larger eggs than nests at similar ocean depths.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"410d1b8f-ee45-495d-849e-db6b446f91b5","content":"<p>The Octopus Garden provides an ideal feeding ground for <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus</em> hatchlings.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["35dd9554-2057-4232-a827-fbf3b54eedde"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the <em>M. robustus</em> population at the Octopus Garden. The text states that the scientists concluded that the site is likely used only for reproduction because over three years they saw many adults, freshly hatched octopuses, and eggs but didn&rsquo;t see any juveniles. This suggests that the <em>M. robustus</em> octopuses that hatch at the Octopus Garden leave the site when they reach an intermediary state of development, returning only as adults for reproductive purposes.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text never discusses the stability of the <em>M. robustus</em> population at the site, only that the scientists observed 6,000 adults, hatchlings, and eggs there. Further, the text presents the site&rsquo;s temperatures as likely beneficial. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t provide any details about the eggs at the site and makes no mention of nests; it indicates only that eggs are present along with hatchlings and adults. Choice D is incorrect because the text makes no mention of the hatchlings feeding at the Octopus Garden, indicating only that the temperatures at the site are probably beneficial and that the site is likely used for reproduction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"16ce90d9","external_id":"5d11fe17-72bb-49af-a3da-3be214ae0c9a","vaultid":null,"uId":"c31eccd5-2586-402a-b936-ad107ae00fdb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2018, scientists discovered an immense aggregation of <em>Muusoctopus robustus</em> (pearl octopuses) along a hydrothermal vent 3,200 meters beneath the ocean&rsquo;s surface. Water temperatures at this site&mdash;named the Octopus Garden&mdash;climb as high as 11&deg;C, much warmer than the ambient 1.6&deg;C typical at this depth. Based on observations made over three years, scientists concluded that temperatures at the site likely confer reproductive benefits and that the site is used exclusively for reproduction&mdash;6,000<em> <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus</em> adults, hatchlings, and eggs were observed at the garden, but no juveniles were present.</p>","stem":"<p>Which statement about <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus</em> and the Octopus Garden is best supported by the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"35dd9554-2057-4232-a827-fbf3b54eedde","content":"<p><em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus </em>leave the Octopus Garden upon reaching an intermediary stage of development.</p>"},{"id":"1230a71b-5811-4147-87a8-49a3c3b5896e","content":"<p>The<em> <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus </em>population at the Octopus Garden remains stable despite variations in water temperature.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3e2a86d1-6489-472a-8b1f-7a522c8525bc","content":"<p><em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus</em> nests in the Octopus Garden contain on average fewer but larger eggs than nests at similar ocean depths.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"410d1b8f-ee45-495d-849e-db6b446f91b5","content":"<p>The Octopus Garden provides an ideal feeding ground for <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"M\">M.</span> robustus</em> hatchlings.</p>"}],"keys":["35dd9554-2057-4232-a827-fbf3b54eedde"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the <em>M. robustus</em> population at the Octopus Garden. The text states that the scientists concluded that the site is likely used only for reproduction because over three years they saw many adults, freshly hatched octopuses, and eggs but didn&rsquo;t see any juveniles. This suggests that the <em>M. robustus</em> octopuses that hatch at the Octopus Garden leave the site when they reach an intermediary state of development, returning only as adults for reproductive purposes.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text never discusses the stability of the <em>M. robustus</em> population at the site, only that the scientists observed 6,000 adults, hatchlings, and eggs there. Further, the text presents the site&rsquo;s temperatures as likely beneficial. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t provide any details about the eggs at the site and makes no mention of nests; it indicates only that eggs are present along with hatchlings and adults. Choice D is incorrect because the text makes no mention of the hatchlings feeding at the Octopus Garden, indicating only that the temperatures at the site are probably beneficial and that the site is likely used for reproduction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669207,"updateDate":1743430669207}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669207, 1743430669207),
